TITLE: Renormalization Group
TAGLINE: The logarithm of Lesson 68 is the integral of a differential equation, μ de/dμ = β(e), and that equation is what survives when the logarithm is no longer small
NUM: 69
NEXT: Lesson 70 returns to the photon field itself and writes the covariant gauge-fixing term that Lesson 37 postponed, so that the Ward identity of Lesson 71 can be stated in a covariant language.

# How to use this lesson

Lesson 68's one-loop formula α(q²)=α/[1−(α/3π)log(|q²|/m²)] is already the solution of a differential equation, though it does not look like one. This lesson writes that equation, names its right-hand side β(e), and shows why changing the scale at which the coupling is defined cannot change a physical prediction. The new object is small in QED and is the whole story in QCD; this course needs it as QED's own bookkeeping. Expect five to seven hours.

## Learning objectives

- Differentiate Lesson 68's one-loop running formula and obtain μ dα/dμ = (2α²)/(3π), hence β(e)=e³/(12π²) for one charged Dirac fermion.

- State the meaning of β(e): the rate at which the renormalized coupling changes with the renormalization scale μ.

- Explain why a physical observable cannot depend on μ, and why that requirement is called a renormalization-group equation.

- Integrate β(e)=e³/(12π²) and recover Lesson 68's logarithm.

- State the sign of β in QED (positive: screening, Landau pole far away) without claiming a result this course has not derived for QCD.

