# 1 From a logarithm to a derivative

## 1.1 Differentiating Lesson 68

Write L=log(μ²/m²), so Lesson 68's formula at scale μ is α(μ)=α/[1−(α/3π)L], with α on the right the low-energy value. Differentiating with respect to log μ, using dL/d log μ=2,

$$ μ dα/dμ = (2α² / 3π) / [1 − (α/3π)L]² = 2 [α(μ)]² / (3π)

to this order, because α(μ)=α/[1−(α/3π)L] makes the denominator square produce exactly α(μ)².

## 1.2 Translating to e

With α=e²/4π, dα=(e/2π) de, so μ de/dμ = (2π/e) μ dα/dμ. Substituting Section 1.1,

$$ β(e) := μ de/dμ = e³ / (12 π²)

for one massless charged Dirac fermion at one loop. (Massive thresholds modify the coefficient piecewise as each lepton's mass is crossed; this course keeps the one-flavor formula.)

### Worked example 1

Check the arithmetic from α=e²/4π and μ dα/dμ=2α²/(3π) to β=e³/(12π²). μ dα/dμ=μ d(e²/4π)/dμ=(e/2π) μ de/dμ. Setting this equal to 2(e⁴/16π²)/(3π)=e⁴/(24π³) gives μ de/dμ=(2π/e)·e⁴/(24π³)=e³/(12π²).

# 2 What the equation is for

## 2.1 Independence of μ

A cross section computed to all orders cannot depend on an arbitrary scale μ introduced only to split Π into Π(0)+Π̂. If a truncated calculation still depends on μ, that dependence is an artifact of the truncation, of order the first omitted term. Requiring μ dσ/dμ=0, with σ depending on μ both explicitly and through e(μ), is a renormalization-group equation.

## 2.2 Using β instead of a large log

If a process involves a large ratio of scales, the logarithm in Lesson 68 is large and α log is not small even though α is. Solving the differential equation of Section 1.2 using the coupling defined at the high scale automatically sums the leading powers of α log, which is a better expansion than expanding in α and then meeting a large log.

### Worked example 2

State, without solving anything, what initial condition the differential equation needs. β determines how e changes with μ, not the value of e at one point; the initial condition is a measured value of α at one scale, Lesson 43's α≈1/137 at low energy, or equivalently a measured α(M_Z) at the Z.

# 3 Integrating back

Separating variables in μ de/dμ=e³/(12π²) gives de/e³ = d log μ / (12π²). Integrating from μ₀ to μ,

$$ 1/e²(μ) − 1/e²(μ₀) = − (1 / 6π²) log(μ/μ₀)

which is Lesson 68's formula after translating e²=4πα and μ²/μ₀² for the logarithm's argument (Exercise 8 fills in the factors of 2). The differential equation and the explicit logarithm are the same statement at this order.

# 4 The sign, and what this course does not claim

β(e)>0 in QED at this order: the coupling grows as μ grows, matching Lesson 68's screening. The Landau pole of Lesson 68, Exercise 16, is the point where this one-loop solution diverges. This course does not compute β for non-Abelian gauge theory and does not claim QCD's opposite sign as a derived result.

