# Solutions

**1.** Let D=1−(α/3π)L with L=log(μ²/m²), dL/d log μ=2. dα/d log μ=α·(α/3π)·2 / D²=2α²/(3π D²).

**2.** α(μ)=α/D so α/D=α(μ) and α/D²=α(μ)²/α·α=α(μ)²/α wait: α/D²=(α/D)² · (D/α)·α / D no. Simply: 2α²/(3π D²)=2(α/D)²/(3π)=2[α(μ)]²/(3π).

**3.** As in Worked example 1: (e/2π)β=2α²/(3π) with α=e²/4π gives β=e³/(12π²).

**4.** β(e) is the rate of change of the renormalized coupling with respect to the logarithm of the renormalization scale.

**5.** μ was introduced by hand as a split in a loop integral; an exact observable cannot remember a bookkeeping choice.

**6.** ∫e⁻³ de = ∫d log μ /(12π²) gives −1/(2e²) = log(μ/μ₀)/(12π²)+c, hence 1/e²(μ)−1/e²(μ₀)=−(1/6π²)log(μ/μ₀).

**7.** e²=4πα so 1/(4πα(μ))−1/(4πα₀)=−(1/6π²)log(μ/μ₀), or 1/α(μ)−1/α₀=−(2/3π)log(μ/μ₀).

**8.** Lesson 68 has 1/α(q²)−1/α=(1/3π)log(|q²|/m²)=(2/3π)log(|q|/m), matching Exercise 7's magnitude with opposite tracking of which scale is larger; the signs agree once μ>μ₀ implies α(μ)>α(μ₀).

**9.** α(μ₀)≈1/137 at a low scale μ₀∼m, or any other measured point.

**10.** Each additional charged fermion adds to the coefficient of β, making the coupling run faster above that fermion's mass.

**11.** α log can be order 1 while α is small; the RG sums those leading logs by using α(μ) at the process scale.

**12.** β>0: charge screened at long distance, larger at short distance.

**13.** 1/e²=0 when log(μ/μ₀)=6π²/e²(μ₀)= (3π/2)/α, same exponential as Lesson 68.

**14.** μ is the point at which the finite part of Π is subtracted, an arbitrary mass scale, not a particle in the spectrum.

**15.** dσ/d log μ = μ∂σ/∂μ + (dσ/de)β(e)=0.

**16.** β=0 would require Π̂ independent of q², hence no running and a momentum-independent finite remainder.

